test('save shows confirmation', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/editor/42');
  await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Save' }).click();

  expect(await page.getByRole('status').isVisible()).toBe(true);
});

scenarios.modelAnswer:
**What is actually failing.** `locator.isVisible()` is an inspection, not a wait: the API reference states that it does not wait for the element to become visible and returns immediately, and its `timeout` option is deprecated and ignored (https://playwright.dev/docs/api/class-locator#locator-is-visible). The `await` only waits for the round trip that answers the question, so the test samples the page at one instant — a few milliseconds after the click in CI, after hydration and the POST on a fast laptop. Nothing about that timing is the product's contract.

**The second defect is the shape of the assertion.** `expect(boolean)` is a generic matcher: it compares two values once. It cannot poll, and wrapping it in anything does not help, because the read has already happened.

**The fix** moves both responsibilities to a web-first assertion:

```ts
test('save shows confirmation', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/editor/42');

  const status = page.getByRole('status');
  await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Save' }).click();

  await expect(status).toHaveText('Saved'); // re-fetches and re-checks until timeout
});
```

`toHaveText` is in the auto-retrying family: it re-resolves the locator and re-checks the condition until it passes or the assertion timeout expires (https://playwright.dev/docs/test-assertions). The retry is now the wait, so no sleep and no retry-until-green is added.

**Why the locator stays role-based.** `getByRole('status')` resolves the live region by its accessibility role, so the test also fails if the element stops being announced — a `div` that loses `role="status"` is a regression, not a refactor. `getByRole('button', { name: 'Save' })` keeps the click on the control a person reads. A test id here would keep passing through exactly those defects.

**If `Saved` is not the contract.** Copy changes and i18n make exact text a brittle target. Two defensible alternatives, in order: assert a stable completion state the product owns — the saved timestamp, the button returning to enabled, or `await expect(page).toHaveURL(/\/editor\/42\?saved=1/)` — or, when the observable is not a DOM assertion (a call count, a value in a store), use `expect.poll` or `expect.toPass`, which retry a callback. The list to avoid is the same either way: `waitForTimeout`, `isVisible()` inside a boolean assertion, and retries as the fix.

**Proving the fix.** Run the spec with `--repeat-each=20` and CI's worker count before merging; one green run on a fast machine proves nothing about the race. If it still fails, the trace is the next thing to read, not a longer sleep.

code.code:
// Editor.test.tsx
import { render, screen } from '@testing-library/react';
import userEvent from '@testing-library/user-event';
import { client } from '../api/client';
import { Editor } from './Editor';

it('saves the document', async () => {
  jest.spyOn(client, 'save').mockResolvedValue({ ok: true } as any);
  render(<Editor documentId="d1" />);

  await userEvent.click(screen.getByRole('button', { name: 'Save' }));
  expect(screen.getByRole('status')).toHaveTextContent('Saved');
});

scenarios.modelAnswer:
**What the mock hides.** `jest.spyOn(client, 'save')` replaces the unit that owns the contract, so everything between the component and the wire leaves the test: URL and method, headers, the `{ data }` unwrapping, the `savedAt` mapping, and the `ApiError` → message mapping. The spy still resolves the old flat `{ ok: true }`, which means the component is being fed a payload the real client no longer produces — the test proves the component can render a status for a shape that no longer exists. It also couples the test to a function name, so a harmless rename breaks it while the envelope change passes silently.

**The boundary that belongs in an integration test is the network:**

```ts
import { setupServer } from 'msw/node';
import { http, HttpResponse } from 'msw';

const server = setupServer(
  http.post('/api/documents/:id', () =>
    HttpResponse.json({ data: { id: 'd1', savedAt: '2024-05-01T10:00:00Z' } })
  )
);

beforeAll(() => server.listen({ onUnhandledRequest: 'error' }));
afterEach(() => server.resetHandlers()); // never leak one test's variant
afterAll(() => server.close());

it('saves and shows the server timestamp', async () => {
  render(<Editor documentId="d1" />);
  await userEvent.click(screen.getByRole('button', { name: 'Save' }));

  expect(await screen.findByRole('status')).toHaveText(/Saved at 10:00/);
});

it('maps a 409 to the conflict banner and keeps the draft', async () => {
  server.use(
    http.post('/api/documents/:id', () =>
      HttpResponse.json({ error: { code: 'CONFLICT' } }, { status: 409 })
    )
  );

  render(<Editor documentId="d1" />);
  await userEvent.click(screen.getByRole('button', { name: 'Save' }));

  expect(await screen.findByRole('alert')).toHaveText(/changed elsewhere/);
  expect(screen.getByRole('textbox')).toHaveValue('draft text');
});
```

The real client runs, so the request it builds must match the handler route; the envelope is unwrapped by production code; the error code travels the real mapping. `onUnhandledRequest: 'error'` turns a forgotten call into a named failure instead of a live request.

**What to keep, and where each belongs.**

- This integration test: the two or three states that matter — success with a timestamp, a mapped error, and the retry that follows it.
- A contract test on the response envelope, because producer and client can still disagree about `data.savedAt`.
- One end-to-end journey for 'a person saves and sees it saved', the only check that the deployed pieces are wired.
- A narrow unit test of the client itself, mocking `fetch`, is where a function-level mock is legitimate: it asserts the URL, method and headers that the integration test only exercises indirectly.

**Proof that the new test can fail.** Break one production line on purpose — read `body.savedAt` instead of `body.data.savedAt`, or drop the 409 branch — and confirm the integration test goes red before reverting. Repeat it for the error mapping. A test that has never been seen failing is a hypothesis, not a check.

code.code:
test('confirm dialog', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Delete' }).click();

  await expect(page).toHaveScreenshot('confirm-dialog.png');

  const scan = await new AxeBuilder({ page }).analyze();
  expect(scan.violations).toEqual([]);
});

scenarios.modelAnswer:
**What this test actually asserts.** Nothing waits for the dialog as a semantic unit. `toHaveScreenshot` retries until it gets a stable image, so it can settle on a pre-open or mid-animation frame, and the axe scan runs on whatever DOM exists at that moment — an empty violation list from the wrong state is worse than no scan, because it reads as evidence. There is also no assertion about the dialog itself: the only role query is the trigger.

**Layer 1 — the semantic contract, which is what the name regression needs.** Locate the dialog by role and accessible name once, and that same line becomes the assertion:

```ts
await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Delete project' }).click();

const dialog = page.getByRole('dialog', { name: 'Delete project?' });
await expect(dialog).toBeVisible();
await expect(dialog.getByRole('button', { name: 'Cancel' })).toBeEnabled();
```

If the name disappears, this fails with 'locator resolved to 0 elements' for `role=dialog[name=...]`: the message names the contract instead of a pixel count, and it fails in the layer that owns the defect. It also settles the dialog before anything else runs.

**Layer 2 — behavior no scan can see.** Escape, focus containment and focus return are the defects that reach users while the DOM looks correct, so they need a keyboard spec against the same settled dialog:

```ts
await page.keyboard.press('Escape');
await expect(dialog).toBeHidden();
await expect(page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Delete project' })).toBeFocused();
```

**Layer 3 — pixels, for the spacing change.** Keep the screenshot, but remove the noise that teaches reviewers to approve diffs blindly: disable animations, mask volatile regions, run it only in the pinned image where the baselines were generated, and keep the diff zero-tolerance once the noise is gone.

```ts
await expect(page).toHaveScreenshot('confirm-dialog.png', {
  animations: 'disabled',
  mask: [page.getByTestId('build-meta')],
});
```

**Layer 4 — the scan, as a safety net with a known shape.** Scope it to the dialog (`new AxeBuilder({ page }).include('[role="dialog"]')`) and treat it as a regression signal for the mechanical subset: missing names, invalid roles, some contrast failures. A rule can flag an unnamed dialog, so do not claim the scan is blind to this class; the point is that its failure arrives as a rule id on a state you happened to render, it cannot judge whether the name is meaningful ('Dialog' passes), and it cannot see Escape working or failing. Automated checks cover a minority of WCAG criteria, so the human pass on the critical flow stays on the release checklist (https://www.w3.org/WAI/test-evaluate/).

**CI review, which is half the design.**

- Baselines are generated only in the pinned image and updated in their own commit; a screenshot diff needs a reviewer from the owning team and a linked design change, otherwise it is approved unread.
- New or missing baselines fail the job instead of being written silently, so a baseline cannot appear as a side effect of a feature PR.
- The accessibility gate blocks on new violations and reports legacy ones with an owner; keyboard specs are part of the suite, not a manual note.
- The semantic assertions are the ones allowed to break a build for a rename; the screenshot breaks for a visual decision.

